---
/* Rich body for the "Redefining webinar customization" case study.
   Scaffolds the page structure with image placeholders to be swapped
   in later. The shared [slug].astro template renders this when the
   slug matches; the default templated layout is bypassed. */
---

<div class="rwc-body">

  {/* ───────────────────── HERO / CONTEXT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-hero" id="case-context">
    <div class="rwc-hero-left">
      <div class="case-cat">Case study · Custom registration fields</div>
      <h1 class="case-title">Redefining webinar customization</h1>
      <p class="case-subtitle">
        As a product designer for StreamYard On-Air webinars, I led the design of
        webinar customization, enhancing the capabilities and flexibility of the
        tool for organizers customizing their webinars.
      </p>

      <div class="rwc-callout">
        <div class="rwc-callout-title">
          <span class="rwc-callout-star" aria-hidden="true">★</span>
          Core achievements
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>1 out of every 3 webinars created used custom registration fields, 4 months after release.</li>
          <li>3× the number of weekly webinars created since releasing webinar customization.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rwc-hero-right">
      <div class="case-img-ph rwc-hero-img is-filled" data-ratio="4-3">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/1.webp" alt="StreamYard hero / registration card" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── MY ROLE + KEY INITIATIVES ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-role" id="case-role">
    <div class="rwc-role-left">
      <div class="eyebrow">My role</div>
      <h2 class="rwc-h2">Senior / Staff Product Designer</h2>

      <p>
        I led the design of the Custom Registration Fields feature, as part of a
        product trio (engineering lead, product manager, and myself). I was
        responsible for researching, conceptualizing, ideating, defining, and
        making sure final designs were delivered according to our specifications.
      </p>
      <p>
        I had weekly syncs with the product trio, as well as with the other
        designers in the company, to foster cross-pod awareness.
      </p>
      <p>
        In parallel to this role, I was also sporadically contributing to the
        Integrations and Video Services teams, Accessibility, and leading the
        StreamYard Design System.
      </p>

      <dl class="rwc-aside">
        <div>
          <dt>My responsibilities</dt>
          <dd>Lead designer, researcher, front-end code reviewer</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>The team</dt>
          <dd>Lead designer, product manager, engineering manager, engineers, director of product</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Duration</dt>
          <dd>4 months</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <aside class="rwc-role-right">
      <div class="rwc-initiatives-title">Key initiatives</div>
      <ul class="rwc-initiatives-list">
        <li>Competitive benchmarking</li>
        <li>Discovery</li>
        <li>Designed product from its inception</li>
        <li>Led research studies</li>
        <li>Informed by data and insights</li>
        <li>Prototyping</li>
        <li>Design system lead</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── DISCOVERY ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-discovery">
    <div class="eyebrow">Discovery</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Uncovering the problem</h2>
    <p class="rwc-lead">
      We conducted extensive research to develop this feature so that we could
      surpass user expectations. We did it through several key activities:
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-3">
      <div>
        <h3>Insights from research, sales, and support teams</h3>
        <p>
          Insights from our customer support, sales teams, and data from weekly
          customer calls were paramount to inform us about what users were
          saying and requesting out of the product. These sources provided
          valuable information on user complaints, suggestions, and usage
          patterns which were critical in shaping the project direction.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>User interviews and concept tests</h3>
        <p>
          We conducted user interviews and concept tests with a diverse group of
          users to understand their overall webinar organizing experience, and
          to capture their needs, preferences, and pain points with the existing
          experience.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Tech reviews to assess feasibility</h3>
        <p>
          We leveraged early ideation mockups with our engineers to explore how
          we could technically implement them. This early involvement with
          engineers was critical to inform what would be feasible during
          definition.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="case-img-ph-grid cols-2">
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/2a.webp" alt="Discovery research artifact" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/2b.webp" alt="Discovery research artifact" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>

  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── THE PROBLEM ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-problem">
    <div class="eyebrow">The problem</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">
      Hosts lacked customization options to be on-brand, and couldn't collect
      registrants' data to fit their needs.
    </h2>
    <p>
      Right after we released StreamYard On-Air webinars, which provided
      barebones customization, our users' requests for extended customization
      made it a priority for us to work on. Customization was just one of the
      key areas where we knew we needed to extend On-Air; the others being
      security, third-party integrations, and engagement.
    </p>
    <p>
      By building custom registration fields and branding for StreamYard On-Air
      webinars, we addressed two of these user needs. Our registration
      experience was currently limited to collecting first name, last name, and
      email — and these 3 fields fell short of providing our users with ways
      of collecting registrant data. We had a clearly validated need for this
      functionality from dozens of customer support and sales calls, as well as
      town hall video comments.
    </p>

    <div class="case-img-ph-grid cols-2">
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/3a.webp" alt="Problem area research board" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/3b.webp" alt="Miro synthesis / ideate board" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="case-img-caption">On-Air webinars registration page, April 2024.</p>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── APPROACH / SOLUTION FRAMEWORK ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-approach">
    <div class="eyebrow">From discovery to solutions</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Finding the right solution</h2>
    <p class="rwc-lead">
      We brought knowledge gathered from our research and tech reviews to make
      sure we designed solutions that could be implemented in time.
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-columns cols-5">
      <div>
        <h3>Eclipse</h3>
        <p>
          We worked with engineers to figure out the technical feasibility of
          every option we wanted to explore, considering scope and tradeoffs.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Market research</h3>
        <p>
          We did extensive market research to learn what competitors and similar
          tools were providing, while finding opportunities to differentiate.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Internal testing</h3>
        <p>
          We ran internal testing with employees across multiple departments to
          gather feedback on early prototypes and concepts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Explore solutions</h3>
        <p>
          We explored multiple solutions and ideas, narrowing down to those that
          best matched user needs and technical feasibility.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Prototype testing</h3>
        <p>
          We tested prototypes with users to validate our solutions, iterating
          quickly based on feedback to ensure we were building the right thing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── STEP 1 — WEBINAR BRANDING ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-step-1">
    <div class="eyebrow">Step 1 · Webinar branding</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">
      Scalability vs. feasibility: WYSIWYG vs. minified previews
    </h2>
    <p>
      Given that customization options for webinars are largely related to look
      and feel, we explored 2 versions of how to provide that scalable
      interaction:
    </p>

    <div class="rwc-options">
      <div class="rwc-option">
        <div class="rwc-option-tag">Option 1</div>
        <h3>Templates with full WYSIWYG</h3>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/4a.webp" alt="Full WYSIWYG template editor exploration" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p>
          We started by trying full WYSIWYG, but this approach required heavy
          engineering investment that didn't match our timeline. The team would
          have had to build essentially a website builder, including a layout
          engine and a robust component library.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-option">
        <div class="rwc-option-tag">Option 2</div>
        <h3>Customizable models, outside of the user flow</h3>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/4b.webp" alt="Minified preview / template-based customization" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <p>
          The second approach used pre-existing templates, customizable in a
          less granular way. Users could select from a set of templates and
          tweak basic properties like colors, fonts, and logos. They wouldn't
          have full layout control, but would still be able to match their
          brand.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* Color customization rules */}
    <div class="rwc-subsection">
      <p>
        We designed a set of rules to guarantee our form was accessible while
        providing full color customization for both buttons and backgrounds. As
        ease-of-use was one of our core principles, we wanted to keep friction
        low — so we decided that one color choice would automatically generate
        the entire webinar palette.
      </p>

      <div class="case-img-ph-grid cols-2">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/5a.webp" alt="Color picker / contrast rules UI" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/5b.webp" alt="Generated color palette example" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        We launched v1 with the minified previews, so that we could get quick
        time-to-value for our users and unlock our engineering team to start
        building custom registration fields faster.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── STEP 2 — CUSTOM REGISTRATION FIELDS ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-step-2">
    <div class="eyebrow">Step 2 · Custom registration fields</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">From a modal to a full page editor</h2>

    <p>
      As we launched the minified previews, I closely collaborated with our
      data analyst and PM to track quant data through Looker, Mixpanel, and
      FullStory sessions. We saw significant usage of webinar branding
      capabilities (78%) within the first 3 weeks, which gave us confidence to
      move toward custom registration fields. Our research led us to agree on
      5 fields to be part of v1: phone number, country, custom text field,
      custom checkbox, and custom single-select.
    </p>

    <h3 class="rwc-h3">
      I reinforced the need for a new single-page UX to house all webinar
      settings.
    </h3>

    <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
      <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/variants.webp" alt="Template variants exploration — WYSIWYG direction" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <p>
      WYSIWYG was the way to go. As we got clarity on our mid- to long-term
      roadmap core initiatives, I pitched the templating concept again to our
      trio and product director, anticipating the need for a more scalable
      information architecture and navigation. A new On-Air settings page
      would house the settings specific to each webinar, organized by
      subpages. It would fit the existing customize-model options, while
      providing a scalable platform for upcoming functionality.
    </p>

    <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
      <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/5c.webp" alt="New On-Air settings page: branding navigation" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <p>
      As we tested and approved internally, we designed custom registration
      fields into their own page.
    </p>
    <ul class="rwc-bullets">
      <li>
        Technically, email, first name, and last name were core to the
        webinar's security, so those 3 fields weren't editable and always
        appeared on top.
      </li>
      <li>Custom fields supported pasting links.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
      <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/6a.webp" alt="Registration fields editor (host) + guest preview" loading="lazy" />
    </div>

    <p>
      We designed the page to be a modular, multi-page registration system that
      could adapt based on whether there was a description, logo, thumbnail, or
      multiple fields.
    </p>

    {/* Responsive breakpoints showcase */}
    <div class="rwc-breakpoints">
      <div class="rwc-breakpoint">
        <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
          <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/7b.webp" alt="Smallest form factor registration view" loading="lazy" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Whichever way users were used to organizing their webinars, we wanted to
      make it the most painless experience possible. By providing both a host
      and a guest view, we made sure the experience was straightforward for
      both ends.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── FINAL RESULT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-final-result">
    <div class="eyebrow">Final result</div>
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">The shipped experience</h2>
    <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-9">
      <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/7c.webp" alt="Final shipped UI showing host and guest views side by side" loading="lazy" />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── VIDEO PRESENTATION ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-video">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Video presentation</h2>
    <div class="rwc-video-embed">
      <iframe
        src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/La_vJZgFhnY"
        title="Know Your Webinar Audience Via Custom Registration Fields"
        loading="lazy"
        allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
        referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
        allowfullscreen
      ></iframe>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ───────────────────── OUTCOMES & IMPACT ───────────────────── */}
  <section class="rwc-section" id="case-impact">
    <h2 class="rwc-h2">Outcomes and impact</h2>

    <div class="rwc-stats">
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">32.9<span class="rwc-stat-unit">%</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Of all webinars used this functionality, 4 months after release.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">6000<span class="rwc-stat-unit">+</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Custom registration fields created, 4 months after release.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="rwc-stat">
        <div class="rwc-stat-n">3<span class="rwc-stat-unit">×</span></div>
        <div class="rwc-stat-desc">
          Increase in weekly webinars created since release.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      Custom registration fields was a success for On-Air webinars, bringing
      new organizers into StreamYard by allowing them to customize their
      webinar registration, being delivered on time, and enhancing the
      flexibility and appeal of StreamYard On-Air webinars. In less than 4
      months since launch, users have created over 6,000 custom registration
      fields for their webinars — demonstrating the feature's utility and
      data-retrieval capabilities for organizers. Since we released this
      functionality, we saw a significant 3× increase of weekly webinars
      created over the following months, from ~200 per week to ~600 per week
      (today). The percentage of registrants that became viewers also gradually
      increased from ~10% to ~15% over time.
    </p>

    <div class="case-img-ph-grid cols-2">
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/8a.webp" alt="Field usage dashboard (Looker)" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
      <div class="case-img-ph is-filled" data-ratio="16-10">
        <img src="/assets/images/cases/sy-webinar/8b.webp" alt="Registrations / viewers trend chart" loading="lazy" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</div>

{/* ───────────────────── IMAGE LIGHTBOX ─────────────────────
   Click-to-zoom for every <img> inside the case body. Single
   overlay element + delegated handler so it picks up any future
   images automatically. */}
<div class="rwc-lightbox" id="rwcLightbox" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="Image preview" hidden>
  <button class="rwc-lightbox-close" type="button" aria-label="Close image preview">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
      <line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"/>
      <line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"/>
    </svg>
  </button>
  <img class="rwc-lightbox-img" alt="" />
</div>

<style>
  /* ===== Shared placeholder styling ===== */
  .case-img-ph {
    border: 2px dashed var(--border-emphasized, var(--border-subtle));
    border-radius: var(--dimension-radius-lg, 12px);
    background: var(--surface-muted, rgba(0,0,0,0.03));
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: var(--dimension-space-24);
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    font-family: var(--font-family-body);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    min-height: 180px;
  }
  .case-img-ph[data-ratio="16-9"]  { aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; }
  .case-img-ph[data-ratio="16-10"] { aspect-ratio: 16 / 10; }
  .case-img-ph[data-ratio="4-3"]   { aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; }
  /* Filled variant: keeps the data-ratio frame and border-radius so
     filled images share a uniform size with placeholders. The image
     is contained inside (no cropping); when the source aspect ratio
     doesn't match the frame, the surrounding background shows
     through as letterboxing. */
  .case-img-ph.is-filled {
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
    background: var(--surface-muted, rgba(0,0,0,0.04));
    /* border-radius and aspect-ratio inherit from .case-img-ph /
       [data-ratio] so frame size matches every other tile. */
  }
  .case-img-ph.is-filled img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: contain;
    border-radius: inherit;
    cursor: zoom-in;
  }
  .case-img-ph-label {
    display: inline-block;
    max-width: 36ch;
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-snug);
  }
  .case-img-ph-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-20);
    margin-top: var(--dimension-space-32);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-16);
  }
  .case-img-ph-grid.cols-2 { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  .case-img-caption {
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-align: right;
    margin-top: var(--dimension-space-8);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-48);
  }

  /* ===== Body wrapper ===== */
  .rwc-body { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .rwc-body p {
    font-family: var(--font-family-body);
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-loose);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-16);
  }
  .rwc-body h2.rwc-h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-family-display);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-black);
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.2rem);
    letter-spacing: var(--font-letter-spacing-tighter);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-heading);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-20);
  }
  .rwc-body h3,
  .rwc-body h3.rwc-h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-family-display);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2vw, 1.35rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-top: var(--dimension-space-32);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-12);
  }
  .rwc-lead {
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg) !important;
    color: var(--text-secondary) !important;
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-32) !important;
  }
  .rwc-body .eyebrow { display: block; margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-12); }

  .rwc-section {
    padding-top: var(--dimension-space-48);
    padding-bottom: var(--dimension-space-48);
    border-top: var(--dimension-border-width-1) solid var(--border-subtle);
  }
  .rwc-subsection { margin-top: var(--dimension-space-40); }

  /* ===== Hero ===== */
  .rwc-hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-48);
    padding-bottom: var(--dimension-space-48);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-16);
    border-bottom: var(--dimension-border-width-1) solid var(--border-subtle);
  }
  .rwc-hero-img { min-height: 320px; }

  .rwc-callout {
    margin-top: var(--dimension-space-24);
    /* Solid surface wash + faint border so the callout reads clearly
       against both light and dark themes. Mix is dialed up so the
       callout sits clearly above the page background. */
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--text-primary) 16%, var(--surface-default));
    border: var(--dimension-border-width-1) solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--dimension-radius-lg, 12px);
    padding: var(--dimension-space-20) var(--dimension-space-24);
  }
  .rwc-callout-title {
    font-family: var(--font-family-display);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-12);
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-8);
  }
  .rwc-callout-star { color: #e6b800; }
  .rwc-callout ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .rwc-callout li {
    font-family: var(--font-family-body);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-loose);
    padding-left: var(--dimension-space-16);
    position: relative;
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-6);
  }
  .rwc-callout li::before {
    content: '•';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    color: var(--text-brand);
  }

  /* ===== Role + Key initiatives ===== */
  .rwc-role {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-48);
    padding-top: var(--dimension-space-48);
    padding-bottom: var(--dimension-space-48);
  }
  .rwc-aside { margin-top: var(--dimension-space-24); display: grid; gap: var(--dimension-space-16); }
  .rwc-aside dt {
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: var(--font-letter-spacing-wider);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-4);
  }
  .rwc-aside dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--font-size-base);
    color: var(--text-primary);
  }
  .rwc-role-right { text-align: center; }
  .rwc-initiatives-title {
    font-family: var(--font-family-display);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-black);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xl);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-bottom: var(--dimension-space-20);
  }
  .rwc-initiatives-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--dimension-space-10);
